          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Themes in Melville's Work</strong><br>\nMelville's writings often delve into the conflicts between society and the individual, good versus evil, and the struggle with personal identity. His works reflect a deep fascination with metaphysical thought.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Other Literary Works</strong><br>\nBeyond novels, Melville also wrote short stories, such as \"Bartleby, the Scrivener\" and \"Benito Cereno,\" showcasing his skill in storytelling and his ability to explore complex themes within shorter narratives.</p>\n\n<p><strong>\"Billy Budd, Sailor\"</strong><br>\nMelville's later work, \"Billy Budd, Sailor,\" was published posthumously. It is a tale of innocence and evil, with the main character, Billy Budd, exemplifying purity in the face of wrongful accusation.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
